      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03 revision 1\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2368828\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 9 August 2019\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address lines: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Schneider Electric Systems USA, Inc., 38 Neponset Avenue, Foxborough, MA 02035, United States of America\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (the example list includes \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\"... An electromagnetic water meter). I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: 6500W, IMT65W (\"Type: 6500W + IMT65W\")\n- Module designation: null (or \"Not applicable\" — not mentioned, so omit)\n- Description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThe certificate covers a family of instruments by meter size DN25 through DN600. Table 2/3 gives characteristics per meter size.\n\nModels: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n\nBut each meter size has multiple variants (accuracy class 1 vs 2 with different Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 and ratio). Let me structure model family as meter sizes, and use matrix tables for the full data plus model_level entries for per-size attributes.\n\nLet me parse Table 2 (page 3):\n\nColumns: Meter size, Accuracy class, Min Q1, Trans Q2, Perm Q3, Over Q4, Ratio Q3/Q1\n\nDN25, class 2:\n- Row A: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400\n- Row B: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400\n\nDN40, class 2:\n- Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.1, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, ratio 400\n- Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n\nDN50, class 2:\n- Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n- Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n\nDN65:\n- class 2: Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.25, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n- class 2: Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250\n\nDN80:\n- class 2: Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n- class 2: Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 160\n- class 1: Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250\n\nDN100:\n- class 2: Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n- class 2: Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160\n- class 1: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n\nDN125:\n- class 2: Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n- class 2: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=1.56, Q2=2.5, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 160\n- class 1: Q1=1.6, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 250\n\nDN150:\n- class 2: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n- class 2: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 160\n- class 1: Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250\n\nDN200:\n- class 2: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=3.94, Q2=6.3, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 160\n- class 2: Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 400\n\nPage 4:\nDN250, class 1: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 160\n\nDN300:\n- class 2: Q1=4, Q2=6.4, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 400\n- class 1: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160\n\nDN350, class 1 or 2: Q1=15.625, Q2=25, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 160\nDN400, class 1 or 2: Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\nDN450, class 1 or 2: Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\nDN500, class 1 or 2: Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\nDN600, class 1 or 2: Q1=63, Q2=100.8, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n\nTable 3 (indicating device):\n- DN25–DN50: Indicating range 99.999.999 m³, Verification scale interval 0.0001 m³\n- DN65–DN150: 99.999.999, 0.001\n- DN200–DN450: 99.999.999, 0.01\n- DN500; DN600: 99.999.999, 0.1\n\nNote: \"99.999.999\" — this uses dots as thousands separators, so it's 99,999,999 m³. As a number that would be 99999999. Hmm, the instruction says use `.` as decimal separator, never `,`. Here the dots are thousand separators. I could record as number 99999999. That's a reasonable interpretation. Or keep as string \"99999999\". I'll use 99999999 as a number.\n\nTable 1 general characteristics:\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic\n- Accuracy class: 1; 2\n- Environmental class: C (fixed meters, installed outdoors)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2\n- Temperature range ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, °C\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C) → value could be \"T50\" with attributes... I'd record water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Actually it says +0,1 °C / +50 °C → min 0.1, max 50. Hmm, \"T50\" class means water temperature range 0.1°C to 50°C. I'll record water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range {min 0.1, max 50, unit °C}.\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1.6 MPa (16 bar) for sizes DN200 and smaller; 1.0 MPa (10 bar) for sizes DN250 and larger. This is config-level (varies by size). Could be config_level with axis \"meter_size\". Or record as two entries. I'll use config_level: attribute maximum_admissible_pressure, axis meter_size, values: [{condition: \"DN200 and smaller\", value: 1.6, unit: MPa}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: 1.0, unit: MPa}].\n- Orientation: All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0D upstream and 0D downstream)\n- Reverse flow: The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow → true / description string\n- Pressure loss class: Δp 63 (0,63 bar) → pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 63\", value 0.63 bar\n- Power supply: Battery 3.6V; External battery pack with output 3.6V; FlexPower 10…30 VDC or 110…230 VAC / 50-60 Hz\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n  - flexpower voltage: DC 10–30 V, AC 110–230 V, frequency 50–60 Hz\n- Software identification / CRC checksum: 5.0.1_/4CB5, 5.0.3_/CFF7, 5.0.2_/71D5, 5.0.5_/DCAB\n\nCharacteristics structure:\n- type_level: measuring_principle, environmental_class, electromagnetic_environment, temperature_range_ambient, water_temperature_class, orientation, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, reverse_flow, pressure_loss_class, power supply bits, software identifications.\n- config_level: maximum_admissible_pressure (axis meter_size).\n- model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class per meter size; verification_scale_interval per size range.\n\nThe model_level is a bit complicated since each meter size has multiple variants (class 1 and 2 rows). To keep it clean, I'll define models per meter size and record model_level for attributes that are single-valued per model. But accuracy class varies per row for DN200 (both 1 and 2 across rows), DN300, and DN350–DN600 (\"1 or 2\"). The flow rates differ per class within a size.\n\nOptions:\n1. Model family models = one per meter size (16 models). matrix_tables preserve full Table 2 rows including multiple rows per size. model_level entries per size where unique (e.g., q4? No, Q4 differs between rows within a size).\n2. Model family models = one per variant row (e.g., DN25-class2-Q3=10, etc.).\n\nGiven the schema example for R137, models were per size and each size had one row. Here, sizes have multiple rows. I think the cleanest approach: models per meter size (DN25...DN600), matrix_tables with the full rows preserving each variant, and model_level entries where values vary by model — I can include the variant-specific data in matrix_tables and use model_level for q3 etc.? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me think about what's most useful. The rule says: \"Matrix detection — MOST IMPORTANT... put varying attributes in model_level, NOT type_level.\" And matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere.\n\nI'll do:\n- model_family.models: one entry per meter size (16 models), model_id \"DN25\" etc.\n- matrix_tables: \n  - \"Flow rates by meter size\" with columns meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; rows for each variant row (28 rows).\n  - \"Indicating device characteristics\" with columns meter_sizes, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval.\n- characteristics.model_level: entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, verification_scale_interval... but since a size may have multiple values for the same attribute (different class variants), model_level values would have multiple entries per model. That's awkward but can be represented with model id including variant disambiguation, e.g. \"DN65 (class 1, Q3=100)\".\n\nAlternative: define model_ids as size+class variants. Let me enumerate the distinct variants:\n\n1. DN25, class 2, Q3=10, ratio 400\n2. DN25, class 2, Q3=16, ratio 400\n3. DN40, class 2, Q3=25, ratio 400\n4. DN40, class 2, Q3=40, ratio 400\n5. DN50, class 2, Q3=40, ratio 400\n6. DN50, class 2, Q3=63, ratio 400\n7. DN65, class 2, Q3=63, ratio 400\n8. DN65, class 2, Q3=100, ratio 400\n9. DN65, class 1, Q3=100, ratio 250\n10. DN80, class 2, Q3=100, ratio 400\n11. DN80, class 2, Q3=160, ratio 400\n12. DN80, class 1, Q3=100, ratio 160\n13. DN80, class 1, Q3=160, ratio 250\n14. DN100, class 2, Q3=160, ratio 400\n15. DN100, class 2, Q3=250, ratio 400\n16. DN100, class 1, Q3=160, ratio 160\n17. DN100, class 1, Q3=250, ratio 250\n18. DN125, class 2, Q3=250, ratio 400\n19. DN125, class 2, Q3=400, ratio 400\n20. DN125, class 1, Q3=250, ratio 160\n21. DN125, class 1, Q3=400, ratio 250\n22. DN150, class 2, Q3=400, ratio 400\n23. DN150, class 2, Q3=630, ratio 400\n24. DN150, class 1, Q3=400, ratio 160\n25. DN150, class 1, Q3=630, ratio 250\n26. DN200, class 2, Q3=630, ratio 400\n27. DN200, class 1, Q3=630, ratio 160\n28. DN200, class 2, Q3=1000, ratio 400\n29. DN250, class 1, Q3=1000, ratio 160\n30. DN300, class 2, Q3=1600, ratio 400\n31. DN300, class 1, Q3=1600, ratio 160\n32. DN350, class 1 or 2, Q3=2500, ratio 160\n33. DN400, class 1 or 2, Q3=4000, ratio 160\n34. DN450, class 1 or 2, Q3=4000, ratio 160\n35. DN500, class 1 or 2, Q3=6300, ratio 160\n36. DN600, class 1 or 2, Q3=6300, ratio 160\n\nThat's 36 variants. Defining 36 model_ids in model_family seems verbose but complete and accurate. Then model_level entries: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class — each with 36 values. That's heavy but follows the schema well.\n\nAlternatively, keep models per meter size and put full detail in matrix_tables. The schema says model_level is \"attribute × model matrix\". Given the ambiguity, I think a middle path: model_family models per meter size (16), matrix_tables capturing every row with meter_size + accuracy_class columns, and model_level for attributes that are single-valued per size: indicating_range? Actually indicating range is same for all (99999999). verification_scale_interval varies by size range.\n\nHmm. But q1..q4 vary by class within size, so they'd be ambiguous in model_level if models are per-size. The matrix_table rows carry the disambiguation via accuracy_class column. I think that's acceptable: matrix_tables preserve the multi-dim table that doesn't fit model_level cleanly, per the schema description (\"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\").\n\nBut the R137 example populated both matrix_tables AND model_level. In R137 each size had one row, so model_level was unambiguous. Here, to follow the spirit, I could use model_id per variant like \"DN65-class1\" vs \"DN65-class2-Q3=100\"... There are two DN80 class-1 rows with different ratios, so \"DN80-class1\" is still ambiguous; need \"DN80-class1-Q3=100\" and \"DN80-class1-Q3=160\".\n\nLet me decide: model_family.models with model_id per variant, labeled like \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" etc. That's 36 models. And model_level with q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 — 5 attributes × 36 values each. Plus accuracy_class 36 values. Plus verification_scale_interval per meter size — I could map sizes to values and repeat per variant.\n\nHmm, this is getting big. Let me think about what's most faithful and useful. The certificate is for a family; Table 2 is the core. I'll go with:\n\n- model_family.models: 16 entries, model_id = \"DN25\"... \"DN600\" (meter size as the model identifier). label maybe null (omit).\n- matrix_tables: two tables (Table 2 flow rates full detail with all 36 rows; Table 3 indicating device).\n- characteristics.model_level: attributes that are unambiguous per size: verification_scale_interval (via Table 3 ranges). For q1..q4/ratio, include them in model_level per variant? That conflicts with 16 models.\n\nAlternatively make model_level entries with model = \"DN65 (accuracy class 1)\" etc. Actually, I think making model per variant is more correct per the \"one entry per variant\" rule: \"model_family: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant... models[]: one entry per variant\". The cert genuinely covers multiple variants per size. The R60 example created model_ids from Emax values — the distinguishing parameter. Here the distinguishing parameters are meter size + accuracy class + flow rate combination.\n\nI'll go with 36 variant models. model_id like \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\"? Or \"DN25 Q3=10 class 2\". Let me create compact ids:\n\n- \"DN25/2/Q3=10\", hmm.\n\nLet me use pattern: `DN<size>-C<accuracy class>-Q3<Q3 value>`. E.g., \"DN80-C1-Q3=100\", \"DN80-C1-Q3=160\". For DN350–DN600 with \"1 or 2\": class \"1or2\". E.g., \"DN350-C1or2-Q3=2500\".\n\nActually cleaner: model_id \"DN350 (class 1 or 2)\". Since one row per size for 350-600, no extra disambiguation needed.\n\nFull list of model_ids:\n1. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\"\n2. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\"\n3. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=25\"\n4. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=40\"\n5. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=40\"\n6. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=63\"\n7. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=63\"\n8. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=100\"\n9. \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\"\n10. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=100\"\n11. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=160\"\n12. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\"\n13. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\"\n14. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=160\"\n15. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=250\"\n16. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=160\"\n17. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=250\"\n18. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=250\"\n19. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=400\"\n20. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=250\"\n21. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=400\"\n22. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=400\"\n23. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=630\"\n24. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=400\"\n25. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=630\"\n26. \"DN200 class 2 Q3=630\"\n27. \"DN200 class 1 Q3=630\"\n28. \"DN200 class 2 Q3=1000\"\n29. \"DN250 class 1 Q3=1000\"\n30. \"DN300 class 2 Q3=1600\"\n31. \"DN300 class 1 Q3=1600\"\n32. \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\"\n33. \"DN400 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\n34. \"DN450 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\n35. \"DN500 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\n36. \"DN600 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\n\nThat's verbose. Hmm, 36 entries × 5 model_level attributes = 180 value objects. That's a lot of JSON but manageable. Actually maybe I should simplify: model_id per meter size only (16 models), and put per-variant flow data in matrix_tables with accuracy_class column. Then model_level for verification_scale_interval only... but then q1..q4 aren't in model_level, which loses the R137-style extraction.\n\nAlternatively: model_level entries with \"model\" referring to a compound label like \"DN65 class 1\". For DN80, both class-1 rows have different q1 (0.625 vs 0.64), q2, q3, q4, ratio — still ambiguous within \"DN80 class 1\". Ugh.\n\nOK decision: go with 36 variant models. It's the most faithful. Let me structure model_level entries:\n\n- accuracy_class: values per variant — string \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\". Unit null.\n- q1 (minimum flow rate): unit m³/h\n- q2 (transitional flow rate): unit m³/h\n- q3 (permanent flow rate): unit m³/h\n- q4 (overload flow rate): unit m³/h\n- ratio_q3_q1: unit null (dimensionless)\n- verification_scale_interval: unit m³ — depends only on meter size: DN25–DN50 → 0.0001; DN65–DN150 → 0.001; DN200–DN450 → 0.01; DN500, DN600 → 0.1.\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³ for all (could be type_level since same for all; but it's per model in Table 3... same value everywhere. I'll put in type_level as indicating_range since it's constant. Hmm, actually Table 3 gives it per size but all values identical. Put as type_level: indicating_range value 99999999 m³. That's cleaner.)\n\nWait, but is \"99999999\" correct? \"99.999.999\" with dots as thousand separators = 99,999,999. Yes.\n\nAlso production locations — not a schema field, but could be mentioned in description or a note. Schema doesn't have production locations. I could add to certified_type description or omit. The schema says don't fabricate fields but production locations are real info. There's no field for it. I could include them in... hmm. Maybe as part of `certified_type.description`? The description field is \"free-text description if present\" — the description present is \"An electromagnetic water meter\". I could add production locations to matrix_tables? No. I'll omit, or add a note. Actually, I might add them to manufacturers[]? No — manufacturers are the certified type's manufacturer (Schneider Electric). Production locations are separate (KROHNE Altometer, KROHNE Shanghai). There's no schema slot. I'll skip them to avoid fabricating structure. Hmm, but losing info... The rules say don't fabricate fields not present; production locations ARE present but no slot exists. I think it's acceptable to add an extra key like \"production_locations\" — but the instruction says \"Return a single JSON object conforming to the layered schema.\" Adding extra keys might violate. I'll include them in certified_type.description as a sentence? That changes description from what's present. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: manufacturers[] could arguably include the production locations since they are locations where the instrument is manufactured. \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations: KROHNE Altometer... KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai)\". These are production sites, effectively manufacturers. Including them as manufacturers[] entries seems reasonable and faithful. I'll include Schneider Electric (applicant and manufacturer) plus the two KROHNE production locations as manufacturers. That preserves the info within schema. I think that's a defensible choice. Alternatively add \"production_locations\" inside certified_type — schema-conformance risk. I'll go with manufacturers including production locations... Hmm, but strictly \"Applicant and Manufacturer Schneider Electric Systems USA, Inc.\" — the label says the manufacturer is Schneider. KROHNE sites are \"production locations\" (subcontracted). Adding them as manufacturers could be considered inaccurate.\n\nGiven the tradeoff, I'll include them as manufacturers with their role clear via... schema doesn't allow a role field for manufacturers. Hmm. Let me just include Schneider Electric as the manufacturer and add the production locations under a \"production_locations\" key in certified_type? No — stick to schema.\n\nAlternative: put in matrix_tables? No.\n\nOK, compromise: include the two KROHNE entities in manufacturers[] — they ARE manufacturers (production locations of the water meter). This preserves info. I'll do that.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\" — canonical \"R49\" (it's R 49-1, part 1). The recommendation is R 49-1 (2006). Canonical id... The example says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, maybe \"R49-1\". I'll use \"R49-1\"? Hmm — id should be canonical recommendation identifier. \"R49\" is the recommendation; -1 is the part. I'll use \"R49-1\" to be precise, edition 2006. Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nHmm, the extraction target may match against a database of R49. \"R49\" is safer for matching? The cert explicitly is \"R 49-1 (2006)\". I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition 2006. Actually let me think: the schema example: id canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For a part-numbered recommendation, canonical might be \"R49-1\". I'll go with \"R49-1\".\n\n- scheme: \"A\" (certificate issued under scheme A).\n- amendment: not mentioned, omit or null. Omit.\n\n**Test reports:**\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01 — 41 pages, 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01 — 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01 — 40 pages, 4 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 — 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- NMi-13200159-01 — 6 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi13200159-02 — 6 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-15200645-01 — 21 pages, 4 annexes\n\nNo dates given for these reports. Role: they're \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated report(s)\" — type_evaluation_report seems right for the R49-1/2006-NL1-xx ones (OIML Type Evaluation Report identified on page 1). The NMi-... ones might be additional test reports. Role field allowed values: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. I'll mark the R49-1/2006-NL1 ones as type_evaluation_report, and NMi- ones as test_report. Documentation folder no. T11652-1 — that's a documentation_file! \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11652-1.\" Include as test_reports entry: id \"T11652-1\", role documentation_file. Pages unknown — omit.\n\nPages field: for R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, \"41 pages and 14 annexes\" → pages: 41. Should annexes be recorded? Schema has id, date, pages, role only. I could add annexes as extra field... stick to schema: pages 41. Hmm, maybe I can include annex count in pages? No. I'll add \"annexes\" as an extra key? The schema lists specific fields; adding could be tolerated but let me keep it clean with just pages. Actually, I'll include annexes info in pages? No. Just omit annexes. Hmm, or I could put pages: \"41 pages and 14 annexes\"? No — keep numeric 41.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- revision \"Initial\", date 25 July 2019, changes \"-\"\n- revision \"1\", date 2 August 2019, changes \"Type mistakes corrected.\"\n\nrevision is string — \"Initial\" and \"1\". Hmm, e.g. \"0\" not 0. \"Initial\" → I'll use \"0\"? The table says \"Initial\". I'll record revision \"Initial\" (string, as present). Actually example: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). Keeping \"Initial\" is faithful. I'll use \"Initial\".\n\n**Footnotes:** None present. Omit.\n\n**Certificate number:** R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03, revision 1. The number field: \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03 revision 1\" — I'll put number \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\" and maybe revision separate? Schema certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. There's no revision field at cert level (revision_history handles it). I'll set number: \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\" and could add \"revision\": \"1\" as extra? I'll include number as full \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\" and rely on revision_history for revision. Maybe include \"number\": \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\", and add \"revision\": \"1\" — extra key... I'll just keep number without revision to stay canonical. Hmm, many pipelines would want the full number. I'll use \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03 revision 1\"? That pollutes matching. Decision: number: \"R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\". \n\n**date_issued:** \"9 August 2019\" → ISO \"2019-08-09\". The schema doesn't specify format; ISO is good.\n\n**oiml_issuer_id:** \"NL1\".\n\n**Scheme:** \"A\".\n\n**page_total:** 5.\n\n**member_state:** \"The Netherlands\".\n\n**Components:** The certified type \"6500W + IMT65W\" — 6500W is the sensor (electromagnetic water meter) and IMT65W likely the indicating device/converter (IMT = indicating measuring transmitter?). Actually Schneider Electric IMT65W... The cert says \"Type: 6500W + IMT65W\". Table 3 mentions \"indicating device\". So components: measuring sensor 6500W and indicating device (electronic calculator) IMT65W? The cert doesn't explicitly assign roles. I could record components:\n- role: \"metering sensor\" — hmm not stated. The cert says \"An electromagnetic water meter. Type: 6500W + IMT65W\". Without explicit roles, safer to record type_designations at certified_type and components with roles \"sensor\" / \"indicating device\" as inferred. Table 3 is \"General characteristics of the indicating device\". I think it's reasonable: component role \"indicating device\" type IMT65W, component role \"sensor\" type 6500W. Actually reverse: 6500W is the flow sensor part? For electromagnetic water meters, typically \"sensor + calculator/converter\". IMT65W is Schneider's electromagnetic flow converter (IMT = ...). Yes, 6500W sensor with IMT65W converter. I'll record components with role \"sensor\" (6500W) and \"indicating device\" (IMT65W), but roles are inferred. Alternatively components[] with role \"certified combination\" and both types? The rule 6: \"For complex instruments... record each as a components[] entry with role, type_designations\". I'll do sensor/calculator with a note that it's inferred? No notes field. I'll use role \"sensor\" and \"indicating device\" — reasonably supported by the doc (indicating device characteristics table exists; the meter is electromagnetic with a sensor).\n\nHmm, actually is it certain 6500W is the sensor and IMT65W the indicating device? Schneider Electric 6500W... Actually the \"Water strainer\"? Let me think: Schneider Electric (formerly Foxboro/Invensys) water meter 6500W — I believe I-Smart 6500W... KROHNE produces the meters (production locations are KROHNE). Schneider + KROHNE: KROHNE OPTIMASS? No. KROHNE Water meter... Schneider Electric IMT65W could be \"Indicating Measuring Transmitter\". Given Table 3 \"indicating device\" and the electromagnetic principle, assigning 6500W = sensor (meter body) and IMT65W = indicating device/converter is sensible. I'll go with it but make roles generic: \"sensor (meter body)\" hmm. Let me just use role: \"flow sensor\" and \"indicating device (converter)\". Keep simple: \"sensor\", \"indicating device\".\n\nActually, to be safe against mislabeling, I could set role: null and just list both type designations as one component? The schema wants role. I'll assign as reasoned.\n\n**Characteristics type_level:**\n\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: [\"1\",\"2\"] — as value list? type_level value should be single. It's already in recommendation.accuracy_classes. Maybe skip in type_level or record value \"1; 2\". I'll record accuracy_class: {value: [\"1\", \"2\"]}? The structured value shape wants value as number|range|string. A list isn't in the shape. Since recommendation.accuracy_classes covers it, skip in type_level. Or record as string \"1; 2\". I'll skip to avoid shape violation... Actually could include as {\"value\": \"1; 2\"}? It's a string value, allowed. Hmm. I'll include it since it's a general characteristic in Table 1. Fine: value \"1; 2\" string? Wait — accuracy classes as string list... I'll omit from type_level since recommendation covers it. Actually no—Table 1 explicitly lists it; redundancy is OK. Let me include value: \"1; 2\". Hmm, but strictness... The shape says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. \"1; 2\" is a string. OK include.\n\nActually, I'll skip it — recommendation.accuracy_classes already captures [1,2]. Keep type_level clean. Hmm... I'll include it, it's harmless. Let me decide: include `accuracy_class: {value: \"1; 2\"}`? No — messy. Skip. Actually... final: skip, rely on recommendation.accuracy_classes.\n\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\"} — with note \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\". Could include description in value string: \"C (fixed meters, installed outdoors)\". Keep value \"C\" and maybe an extra characteristic environmental_class_description? I'll do value \"C\" and put parenthetical into a separate `environmental_class_note`? Simpler: value: \"C (fixed meters, installed outdoors)\"? I'll use value \"C\" plus add \"installation\": \"fixed 