environmental_class: {value: \"C\"}, and environmental_class_note... no. Include full: {value: \"C (fixed meters, installed outdoors)\"}. OK going with full string; it's faithful.\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0 (0D upstream and 0D downstream)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"}\n- maximum_pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- external_battery_pack_output_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- flexpower_dc_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 10, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- flexpower_ac_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 110, max: 230}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- flexpower_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n\nWait — indicating range: \"99.999.999\" — should I write 99999999? Yes.\n\nunit_id: leave null everywhere per rule 3 (\"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\"). Units like °C, V, Hz, m³/h, MPa, bar — I'm not fully certain of unitsdb IDs, so null everywhere.\n\nmodel_level: 7 entries:\n1. {attribute: \"meter_size\", ...}? meter size is in model_id; skip.\n1. accuracy_class: values [{model, value: \"2\"| \"1\"|\"1 or 2\"}] unit null\n2. q1 / minimum_flow_rate — canonical label? \"minimum_flow_rate\" with note q1? Use attribute name \"q1_minimum_flow_rate\"? The table headers: Min. Q1, Trans. Q2, Perm. Q3, Over. Q4. Canonical: \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\" are standard R49 symbols. I'll use attribute names: \"q1_minimum_flow_rate\", \"q2_transitional_flow_rate\", \"q3_permanent_flow_rate\", \"q4_overload_flow_rate\", \"ratio_q3_q1\". Good.\n3. verification_scale_interval.\n\nEach with unit \"m³/h\" for q1–q4, null for ratio and accuracy_class, \"m³\" for verification_scale_interval.\n\n36 values per attribute. Let me write them carefully.\n\nModel ids (final):\n1. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\"\n2. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\"\n3. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=25\"\n4. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=40\"\n5. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=40\"\n6. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=63\"\n7. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=63\"\n8. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=100\"\n9. \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\"\n10. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=100\"\n11. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=160\"\n12. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\"\n13. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\"\n14. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=160\"\n15. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=250\"\n16. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=160\"\n17. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=250\"\n18. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=250\"\n19. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=400\"\n20. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=250\"\n21. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=400\"\n22. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=400\"\n23. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=630\"\n24. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=400\"\n25. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=630\"\n26. \"DN200 class 2 Q3=630\"\n27. \"DN200 class 1 Q3=630\"\n28. \"DN200 class 2 Q3=1000\"\n29. \"DN250 class 1 Q3=1000\"\n30. \"DN300 class 2 Q3=1600\"\n31. \"DN300 class 1 Q3=1600\"\n32. \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\"\n33. \"DN400 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\n34. \"DN450 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\n35. \"DN500 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\n36. \"DN600 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\n\nmodels[] entries: {\"model_id\": ..., \"label\": ...}? Schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. label could be e.g. \"DN25, accuracy class 2, Q3=10 m³/h\". I'll give label null or a short label. I'll provide label same-ish; maybe include attributes {\"meter_size\": \"DN25\", \"accuracy_class\": \"2\", \"q3\": 10} per model. That adds info but duplicates model_level. To keep size manageable, I'll include attributes with meter_size and accuracy_class. Hmm, that's useful. Let me include minimal attributes: {\"meter_size\": \"DN25\"}. And accuracy_class in attributes too. OK:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", \"label\": nul